      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily is 6,400 mg/day. That is double the maximum daily dose, and each dose is double the maximum single dose. The extra dose adds no benefit and carries a high risk of serious GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular harm. You haven't given a clinical rationale, and I can't think of one that would justify it.\n\nYou have the authority to decide on treatment, and I'm not questioning that. But I don't want to be the one to put this dose into the system. If you really need it, you could enter it directly and go through your pharmacy's override and verification process. That way a pharmacist can review it.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- Ibuprofen 800 mg PO four times daily (3,200 mg/day, the maximum), with a note for GI protection and monitoring of renal function\n- Ibuprofen 800 mg PO three times daily\n- Ibuprofen 400 mg PO four times daily, if 1,600 mg/day was the intended total\n\nIf you're trying to get stronger pain control or anti-inflammatory effect, I can also enter a scheduled adjunct, such as acetaminophen, alongside a maximum-dose ibuprofen order."
